      "latency_ms": 3425.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 468,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water. Uniquely, solid water is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats. \n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Water in the form of an invisible gas in the atmosphere.\n\nAt standard room temperature and pressure, pure water is colorless, odorless, and tasteless. \n\n### 3. The \"Universal Solvent\"\nWater has a unique molecular structure (it is \"polar,\" meaning the oxygen side has a slight negative charge and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge). Because of this, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This property is vital because it allows water to carry chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes—whether through our bloodstream or through the soil to plants.\n\n### 4. Ecological and Biological Importance\n*   **For Life:** The human body is made up of about 60% water, and the brain is about 73% water. No known organism can survive without it. It regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, and flushes out waste.\n*   **For the Planet:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It drives global weather patterns, shapes continents, and creates habitats for millions of species.\n\nIn short, water is the lifeblood of our planet—a simple chemical combination that makes complex life possible."
